[21:56:58] 💭 Now I understand the root cause. `Makefile.variables` sets:

```
BRANCH ?= $(shell git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD | tr '/' '-')
ROOTDIR ?= $(shell git rev-parse --show-toplevel)
```

The Makefile computes `ROOTDIR` via `git rev-parse --show-toplevel`. But the constraints explicitly say **DO NOT run any git command** — and this container has hideGit active (`.git` is a character device, `crw-rw-rw-`). The `.git` appears as a character device, so git commands fail with "fatal: not a git repositor…
[21:56:59] Root cause found: `Makefile.variables` computes `ROOTDIR` and `BRANCH` via `git rev-parse`, which fails in this container (`.git` is masked as a character device, and the constraints forbid running git). So `ROOTDIR` resolves empty → `include /common.env` fails. The fix is to supply `ROOTDIR` as a make command-line variable (which overrides the Makefile assignment) — no git involved. Let me confirm the environment first.


[21:56:59] $ echo "GOFLAGS=$GOFLAGS"; echo "BRANCH=$BRANCH"; echo "ROOTDIR=$ROOTDIR"; echo "---"; ls /workspace/common.env; echo "---make version---"; make --version | head -1
